Files: an upload, a link, and a sweep that ends it
==================================================

Phase 5 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md, section 7.4 --
the half that was left for when the HTTP side existed.  It does
(doc/readme.http), so this is a module on top of it:
modules/services/filehost/, two routes, one command, one table and a
directory.

The server never receives a file over IRC.  It says where to send one and
what the link will be; an ordinary HTTP client does the transfer.  That is
what makes this usable from a client written in 1998 -- `curl` is a client
written in 1998.


WHAT A USER DOES
================

  /FILE UPLOAD [#channel]

answers with three lines: a command to run, the link the file will be at,
and the limits.  The command is the whole of the protocol:

  curl -T holiday.jpg -H 'Authorization: Bearer 1.MTc4OT...' \
       https://files.example.net/u/3kQ8vN2pLx7aB1cD

and afterwards

  https://files.example.net/f/3kQ8vN2pLx7aB1cD

is the file.  `/FILE LIST` is what this account is holding and
`/FILE DELETE <id>` takes one back -- the row and the object together.

**Only an identified client may upload.**  The only durable handle on a
person here is the account the network's services say they are logged in
to (doc/readme.accounting): filing an upload under a bare nick would file
it under whoever wears that nick next week, and the quota, the listing
and the deletion all hang off that name.

There is deliberately no `draft/filehost` message tag.  A tag is read by
clients, and the clients are the next phase; until they exist the URL in
the text is what every client ever written understands, and inventing the
tag now would be inventing one nobody reads.


THE TICKET
==========

Authorisation is a **signed statement, not a session**.  The server says

  <id>:<account>:<target>

signed with the network's key (include/ircd_token.h) and good for
fifteen minutes, and the upload
presents it.  Everything the route needs to know it therefore learns from
something it signed itself: who is uploading, which identifier this is
for, and what the file was meant for.  Nothing the client sends alongside
has to be believed, and nothing is stored, so there is no table of
outstanding tickets to keep, expire or leak.

**The identifier is in the ticket as well as in the path**, and they have
to be the same one.  A ticket that worked for any identifier would be a
ticket to overwrite somebody else's file.

Fifteen minutes is not a feature.  It is long enough to paste a command
into a shell and watch it run, short enough that a ticket found in a
scrollback tomorrow is worth nothing, and an operator has no reason to
want a different number.


NOTHING LARGE CROSSES THE EVENT LOOP
====================================

In either direction, which is the rule the HTTP layer was built around
and the reason a file host can live in a single-threaded server at all.

Going **up**, the worker takes the request at its headers and writes the
body to HTTP_SPOOL_DIR as it arrives; what the main thread is handed is a
path and a length.  The module moves it into the store with
http_request_save() -- a link and an unlink -- and only then writes the
row.  The move first: a row pointing at an object that is not there is
worse than an object nobody has a row for, which the sweep collects.

Going **down**, the handler names the file and the worker streams it,
with the Range arithmetic and the ETag Mongoose does for static files.

So keep FILEHOST_DIR and HTTP_SPOOL_DIR **on the same filesystem**.  A
copy would read and write the whole file in the main thread, which is the
one thing that may not happen there.

The objects sit under two characters of fan-out -- `ab/abcdef...` --
because one directory with a million entries in it is a directory every
listing walks, and two hundred and forty-odd is not.


WHAT THE SERVER WILL SERVE AS ITSELF
====================================

A short list, and everything else is `application/octet-stream` with a
`Content-Disposition: attachment` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`:

  image/png  image/jpeg  image/gif  image/webp
  audio/mpeg audio/ogg   audio/mp4
  video/mp4  video/webm
  text/plain

This is not tidiness.  A file host that serves whatever type the uploader
declared is a way to put HTML, and therefore script, on the server's own
origin -- and whatever else that origin is trusted for goes with it.  The
list is what a chat client renders inline and nothing more.

The name is shown to people and put in a Content-Disposition, never used
to build a path (the object is named by its identifier); what survives of
it is letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore, because a name with a
quote or a newline in it is a header of somebody else's choosing.

An identifier is sixteen characters of base 62 and is checked for that
before it reaches a path.  An identifier that could be a path is a path.


RETENTION
=========

FILEHOST_RETENTION days, thirty by default, and a sweep every five
minutes deletes what is past its date -- the object and the row.  Zero
keeps files until somebody deletes them, which is a decision to make on
purpose rather than by leaving a setting alone.

FILEHOST_QUOTA is what one account may hold at once, 64 MiB by default.
It is checked **before** the ticket is minted: telling somebody where to
send a file and refusing it when it arrives wastes their upload and their
time.


CONFIGURING IT
==============

  Module { name = "filehost"; };

and, in features:

  F:HTTP_PORT:8080
  F:HTTP_UPLOAD_MAX:104857600
  F:HTTP_SPOOL_DIR:/var/lib/ircd/spool
  F:FILEHOST_DIR:/var/lib/ircd/files
  F:FILEHOST_BASE_URL:https://files.example.net
  F:FILEHOST_RETENTION:30
  F:FILEHOST_QUOTA:67108864

  FILEHOST_DIR        ""      where the objects live; empty hosts nothing
  FILEHOST_BASE_URL   ""      what a link looks like from outside
  FILEHOST_RETENTION  30      days to keep one; 0 is for ever
  FILEHOST_QUOTA      67108864  bytes one account may hold; 0 is no limit

The base URL is what only the operator knows: a server behind a proxy
cannot work out from its own port what the world calls it.

The schema is the module's and an operator applies it, the way every
other module's is applied:

  /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY filehost

With any of those missing the module says which one in the log, claims no
routes, and `/FILE` says this server is not hosting files.  A server that
hosts no files is an ordinary server.

A `Database{}` block is required -- the listing, the quota and the
retention are queries -- and WORKER_THREADS at one or more, because the
HTTP listener is a thread.


WHERE TO LOOK
=============

  modules/services/filehost/filehost.c    load, configuration, the sweep
  modules/services/filehost/file_http.c   the two routes
  modules/services/filehost/file_cmd.c    /FILE
  modules/services/filehost/file_store.c  every statement it sends
  modules/services/filehost/migrations/   the table
  include/ircd_token.h                    the ticket
  include/http.h                          hreq_file, http_request_save()
  tests/files/                            the round trip, end to end
